  "strategy": "# Central Approach: Rough Valley optimization with mode switching\nThe key idea of this strategy is to perform:\n1. Strong exploitation of the neighborhood of the parent solution\n2. Broad search based purely on diverse Dirichlet initialization patterns\n3. Genetic propagation of promising patterns while maintaining micro-optimization\n\nThis approach specifically uses a hybrid search method where:\n- Primary focus is local optimization using projected gradient around parent patterns\n- Secondary use of genetic crossover with noise between patterns\n- Adaptive time budget switching between exploitation and exploration phases\n\nThe rationale is that nonnegative sequences for minimax convolution are often concentrated at few \"modes\" including:\n- Center weights\n- Edge weights\n- Alternating weights\n- Uniform weights\n\nSuccessful candidates usually emerge from focusing mass near approximative solutions of a weighted combination of peaks.\n\n## Mathematical Specification\nThe optimization seeks to minimize:\n    obj = (2 * n * max(auto_convolve(a))) / (sum(a))^2\n\nwith two key invariants:\n1. Nonnegative coefficients (a_i >= 0 for all i)\n2. Sum normalization (sum(a) >= 0.01)\n\nwhere auto_convolve(a) computes the linear convolution (full mode) which ranges from 0 to 2*n-2.\n\nThe optimization uses:\n- Standard simplex projection keeping sum normalization\n- Analytical gradient calculation for fixed penalization T>0\n- A running set of parent patterns that guide exploration\n\n## Implementation Procedure\n### Setup\n1. **Initializations**: Generate diverse initial patterns from two sources:\n   - Projected parent (if available) with strength speeds 0.5 and 0.3\n   - Uniform, spiked, two-hump, and mirrored base patterns\n\n2. **Time Budget**: Maximum 1800 seconds total. We allocate:\n   - Primary exploitation time: 50% of budget (900s)\n   - Secondary broad search time: 50% of budget (900s)\n\n### Core Optimization Loop\nSet defaults:\n```\nparent = None\nn_init = 256\n```\n\nIf parent is given, use it as starting point with slight perturbations.\n\nPhase 1: Primary exploitation (1500s remaining)\n```\n// Perform 30 rounds of combined optimization\n1. Local coordinate descent around parent\n2. Global projected gradient optimization\n3. Multi-start simplex with increasing micro-steps\n```\n\nPhase 2: Secondary broad search (full remaining budget)\n```\n// Generate diverse initializations and run global optimization\n1. Start from set of 5 unique initialization patterns for n=384\n2. For each pattern, optimize with projected gradient and simplex projection\n3. Track best candidate and entropy state\n4. Periodically inject new patterns from crossover of current best candidates\n```\n\n### Termination\nContinue search until:\n1. Time budget exceeded\n2. Improvement plateau detected (when no significant optimization occurs for 500 seconds)\n3. Optimization library says no more improvements\n\nAt each step, save the best candidate and its score with the original evaluator to avoid invalid corners.\n\n## Search Choices\n**Length strategies**:\n- Fallback to 384 if parent length is incompatible\n- Maintain a single fixed length throughout optimization (384) unless specified by parent\n\n**Pattern exploration**:\n- Generate patterns at:\n   - Base length (384)\n   - Base length \u00b1 30% (288 to 480)\n\n**Optimization Techniques**:\n- Use a custom combination of:\n   - Coordinate-wise gradient descent on simplex\n   - Linear regularization for keeping numerical stability\n   - Weighted inversion around potential peak points\n\n**Core Updates**:\n- Maintain a set of up to 10 best candidates with replacement\n- Each update:\n   1. Project current candidate to simplex if needed\n   2. Apply gradient descent with step size and regularization\n   3. Evaluate using original objective\n\n## Budget Plan\n- Primary exploitation: 900 seconds for deep optimization\n- Secondary exploration: 900 seconds for broad search\n- Final verification: 300 seconds\n\n## Validation\nAfter each evaluation, save the best candidate independently. Perform numerical checks:\n- Maximize the minimal possible score\n- Ensure all coefficients remain nonnegative\n- Sum normalization protects against invalid optimization",
